  "summary": "An internal gear pump has two gears of different sizes: an outer rotor with internal teeth, driven by the shaft, and a smaller idler with external teeth that runs on a fixed pin set off-centre. A crescent-shaped partition fills the space between them on the side away from the mesh. As the gears turn, teeth separate at the inlet and draw liquid in; it is carried around in pockets above and below the crescent and squeezed out as the teeth re-mesh at the outlet. Low shear, low speed and good suction make it ideal for viscous liquids.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Positive displacement – rotary",
    "configuration": "Internal gear, crescent type (\"gear-within-a-gear\")"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "rotor_teeth": 12,
    "idler_teeth": 8,
    "module_mm": 8.0,
    "idler_speed_ratio": 1.5,
    "note": "Many commercial designs use pin-type rotor teeth; involute form shown for clarity."
  },
